    "narrative": "This program performs exactly two syscalls: **sys_write** to file descriptor 1 (stdout) and **sys_exit** with code 0. The write call transfers 14 bytes from a read-only data section to stdout — the string `Hello, World!\\n`. No file access, no network activity, no dynamic memory allocation, and no privilege changes are observed.\n\nThe register setup follows the standard Linux x86-64 syscall calling convention precisely: RAX holds the syscall number, RDI/RSI/RDX carry arguments 1/2/3. The `xor rdi, rdi` instruction before `sys_exit` is a common size-optimization idiom (3 bytes vs 7 for `mov rdi, 0`).\n\nThis is the canonical minimal x86-64 ELF program. It is completely safe and serves as the reference baseline for all behavioral comparisons.",
